<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  v108.0 — home-screen widget palette, LIGHT.

  v107.0 kept the card navy in BOTH themes on the grounds that the in-app goal
  card is navy in light mode too. Steven asked for the widget to follow the
  system theme instead, so it now does: cream card with navy ink in the day,
  charcoal card with light ink at night (see values-night).

  Note for future edits: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so the
  CSS custom-property names cannot be written here in their real form.

  THE ACCENT IS NOT THE RAW BRAND AMBER, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Brand amber on
  this cream scores 4.05:1, which FAILS WCAG AA for normal text (needs 4.5:1) —
  it was only ever used on navy, where it is fine. The darkened value below is
  the same hue at 5.47:1. Reusing the brand value here would have shipped a
  headline number that fails contrast on the exact surface it was moved onto.

  Every value below is pinned by the contrast block in test-widget.js, which
  READS THIS FILE — so an edit that breaks contrast fails the suite rather than
  the user's eyes.

  Every one of these is referenced from layout XML, never from Java: RemoteViews
  are inflated in the launcher's process, so a colour resolved in ours would
  ignore the system theme entirely. The bar bitmap is the one exception — it
  needs a Canvas — and it reads widget_bar_* from OUR resources, which is why
  those exist under separate names: see WidgetRenderer.barColors().

  On API 31+ these are overridden by values-v31 (Material You). This file is the
  fallback for API 24-30, and the definition of the brand-coloured look.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Card surface + text. -->
    <color name="widget_bg">#F8F4ED</color>
    <color name="widget_ink">#1C2A44</color>
    <color name="widget_ink_dim">#5B6880</color>
    <color name="widget_accent">#A8432A</color>
    <color name="widget_track">#DFD7C8</color>
    <color name="widget_good">#1E7A4B</color>

    <!-- Week-bar chart. Resolved in OUR process (Canvas), so they are named
         separately from the layout colours to keep that boundary visible.
         `current` is the week in progress, deliberately darker rather than a
         different hue so the chart still reads as one series. -->
    <color name="widget_bar_fill">#A8432A</color>
    <color name="widget_bar_current">#7A2E1B</color>
    <color name="widget_bar_track">#DFD7C8</color>
</resources>
